==============================================================================
1. THE ORACLE: sampled CPU ns / EXACT CPU ns (same window, same
   definition of CPU). /proc shown only as context -- see the caveat.
==============================================================================
rate tree    sampled Gns     exact Gns  sampled/EXACT    sampled//proc
  10 base           90.0         152.9          0.588            0.551
  10 fix           116.9         152.2          0.768            0.723
  50 base           87.3         150.6          0.580            0.537
  50 fix           124.0         150.0          0.826            0.766
 100 base           88.7         154.1          0.575            0.543
 100 fix           119.7         150.5          0.795            0.761
 200 base           95.4         151.1          0.632            0.592
 200 fix           134.6         152.8          0.881            0.826

CAVEAT on the //proc column: neither tier's ratio to /proc is an oracle.
Both tiers call a backend on-CPU when NO wait event is registered, which
on an oversubscribed box also counts run-queue delay; /proc utime+stime
counts only time actually on a core; and each tier separately excludes
15-20% of its own we==0 as out-of-command. The exact tier's ~0.93 is
offsetting errors, not accuracy, so it was never a reachable target.

==============================================================================
2. UNGATED we==0: is any gate-order variant left?
   ungated = that tier's CPU + that tier's own gate-rejected CPU.
==============================================================================
rate tree  ungated_ex% ungated_sa% ungated_pp  gated_pp  ex_rej%ofwe0  sa_rej%ofwe0
  10 base         49.2        42.9       6.3      14.0          19.1          43.0
  10 fix          43.1        35.8       7.3       7.2          17.3          18.5
  50 base         42.3        36.6       5.7      12.5          20.1          44.0
  50 fix          41.9        36.3       5.6       5.4          19.4          20.1
 100 base         42.5        36.8       5.7      12.8          17.2          42.3
 100 fix          44.9        38.5       6.4       6.5          15.1          16.2
 200 base         42.4        38.0       4.5      10.8          20.1          41.8
 200 fix          42.0        37.5       4.4       3.9          20.1          19.2

Reading: on BASE the gate roughly DOUBLES the disagreement (ungated
4.5-6.3 pp -> gated 10.8-14.0 pp) because the sampler rejects 42-44% of
its own we==0 while the exact tier rejects 17-20%. On FIX the two
rejection shares agree and ungated ~= gated, so nothing gate-ordered is
left; the remaining 4.4-7.3 pp is upstream of the gate, in how often the
sampler observes we==0 at all (the wait-read path).

==============================================================================
3. ITEM 4: did the recovered CPU become an attribution hole?
==============================================================================
rate recovered recovered_Gns   d_unattributed_Gns     d_backfilled_Gns  pend_overflow
  10       352        35.20               0.0009                0.421              0
  50      1901        38.02              -0.0023                0.455              0
 100      3529        35.29               0.0045                0.935              0
 200      7575        37.88              -0.0008               -0.298              0

The live unattributed bucket is 3-5 MILLIseconds in both trees, so the
delta is ~0.000 Gns against 35-38 Gns recovered: 0.00% of the recovered
CPU lands there, and pending_overflow is 0 everywhere, so the #128
deferred-attribution path absorbed all of it. Mechanism agrees: for an
at-risk target the pre-recheck query_id was ALREADY 0 (the tick
derivation zeroes it while cmd_open is false), so the recheck cannot
make those samples' attribution worse than it already was -- and before
the fix they did not exist at all.

NOT clean, stated rather than hidden: summary_query_unattributed_ns_total
(the SUMMARY writer's bucket, which has no deferral) swings in BOTH
directions between trees and does not track the recovered volume, which
is ~constant at 35-38 Gns across rates:
     10 Hz base     8.33 Gns -> fix     4.00 Gns (delta    -4.34)
     50 Hz base     8.23 Gns -> fix     7.23 Gns (delta    -1.00)
    100 Hz base     1.73 Gns -> fix    21.77 Gns (delta   +20.04)
    200 Hz base     4.28 Gns -> fix     9.42 Gns (delta    +5.13)
   At n=1 per cell no part of that is attributable to this fix; it is
   also not shown to be unrelated. Flagged, not claimed either way.
